The `test_ram` assertion fails because the expected value `RAM_EXPECT` (0x01020304) does not match the actual value obtained from iterating the `test_ram` section. The `test_ram` section is configured to be sorted alphabetically by symbol name.

The current declarations are:
- `STRUCT_SECTION_ITERABLE(test_ram, ram3) = {0x03};`
- `STRUCT_SECTION_ITERABLE(test_ram, ram2) = {0x01};`
- `STRUCT_SECTION_ITERABLE(test_ram, ram4) = {0x04};`
- `STRUCT_SECTION_ITERABLE(test_ram, ram1) = {0x02};`

When sorted alphabetically by symbol name (`ram1`, `ram2`, `ram3`, `ram4`), the values would be `0x02`, `0x01`, `0x03`, `0x04`. Concatenating these values as `(out << 8) | t->i` would result in `0x02010304`.

The `RAM_EXPECT` is `0x01020304`. To match this expectation with alphabetical sorting, the values assigned to `ram1` and `ram2` need to be swapped.
- `ram1` should have value `0x01`.
- `ram2` should have value `0x02`.
- `ram3` should have value `0x03`.
- `ram4` should have value `0x04`.

This change will ensure that when the items are iterated in alphabetical order (`ram1`, `ram2`, `ram3`, `ram4`), the collected `out` value becomes `0x01020304`, matching `RAM_EXPECT`.

